# Hide elements and keep them accessible to screen readers

Use visually hidden text to give screen reader users context that sighted people get from the layout. Only a maintained custom card can do this. Home Assistant has no dashboard setting for it.

## Hiding in a dashboard hides it from everyone

A visibility condition removes a card, badge or section from the screen and from screen readers. So do options such as `hide_state` and `show_name: false`. [Override how elements display](structure-display-overrides.html) explains these settings.

So give every native card a visible name that makes sense on its own. For example, name 2 Tile cards **Kitchen light** and **Hall light**, not **Light** twice. Then nobody needs hidden text to tell them apart.

## Visually hidden text in a custom card

In a maintained custom card, use a visually hidden class. It keeps the text in the page for screen readers but takes it off the screen. This is the class from the civica house lighting card.

```css
.vh {
  position: absolute;
  width: 1px;
  height: 1px;
  overflow: hidden;
  clip-path: inset(50%);
  white-space: nowrap;
}
```

The house lighting card uses it in 2 places:

- an icon-only settings button, which screen readers announce as "Settings for Kitchen"
- an error message, which starts with "Error:" so screen reader users know it is an error and not a hint

```html
<button type="button" class="btn btn--secondary">
  <svg aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">…</svg>
  <span class="vh">Settings for Kitchen</span>
</button>
```

## What not to hide

- Do not use `display: none` or the `hidden` attribute for text that screen readers need. They hide it from everyone.
- Do not visually hide a button, link or field. Someone using a keyboard would move focus to something they cannot see.
- Do not hide text that sighted people need too. If the name helps everyone, show it.
- Add `aria-hidden="true"` only to decorative icons that sit next to a text label.
